  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 152 PM EDT Sat Oct 24 2026\n\n- Fog Possible Late Tonight into Sunday Morning\n\nThere is another risk for fog beginning late tonight especially east \nof US 131. NBM hourly guidance indicates dew point depressions near \nzero, RH values close to 100%, and wind speeds less than 5 mph. HRRR \nsoundings at LAN and JXN show solid low level temperature inversions \nwith light winds through the stable layer. As a result, it seems \nprobable that at least patchy if not numerous areas of fog may \ndevelop once again tonight mainly closer to 06z and possibly lasting \nto 14z or so. The best risk is near and east of a Big Rapids to \nBattle Creek line.\n\n- Cooler Early Week Period, Then Warming Again\n\nRelative to the temperatures we've had over the last week, cooler \nreadings move in Monday into Tuesday as a cold front swings through \nthe state and winds shift out of the north and then northeast. This \nfront should move through dry. Highs across central Lower MI dip \ninto the mid to upper 50s each day while southern Lower MI stays \nmore in low to mid 60s.\n\nFrom Wednesday through Halloween, ensemble guidance favors upper 60s \nto low 70s each day, well above normal for the end of October.\n\n- Chance for Showers Late Week\n\nAn increasing low level jet late Wednesday ahead of a trailing cold \nfront associated with a deepening low over Ontario may bring some \nshowers to the region as early as late Wednesday, lingering into \nThursday. Probabilities are currently 20-40% for this to occur, with \nthe highest likelihood near and west of US 131.\n\nToo early to determine if Halloween may stay dry or not. Ensemble \nguidance's individual member QPF shows a more active signal late \nnext weekend into early November for a rather wet pattern. Whether \nthis impacts Halloween remains to be seen. A period of anomalously \nhigh PWAT values may move in by late next weekend with perhaps \nenough instability to support a chance for thunderstorms.",
